    "https://jobs.cofc.edu/postings/18798": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor of Historic Preservation and Communitry Planning (interior design studio)",
      "description": "College of Charleston Employment Site | Adjunct Instructor of Historic Preservation and Communitry Planning (interior design studio) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Applicant Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Academic Jobs Student Jobs Contact Us Adjunct Instructor of Historic Preservation and Communitry Planning (interior design studio) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following through the College of Charleston PeopleAdmin system: A letter of application describing interest in teaching HPCP 340: Special Topics, Interior Design Studio and relevant professional and/or teaching experience; A current CV or résumé; Unofficial graduate transcript(s); A professional portfolio or portfolio website demonstrating relevant design work; and Names and contact information for three professional references. Teaching evaluations or examples of prior teaching may be included if available but are not required. Review of applications will begin 16 November and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to: Dr. R. Grant Gilmore III Director, Historic Preservation & Community Planning College of Charleston gilmorerg@cofc.edu Posting Details General Information Rank Adjunct Instructor FLSA Exempt Position Type Adjunct Internal Title Adjunct Instructor of Historic Preservation and Communitry Planning (interior design studio) Department Art and Architectural History School/Area School of the Arts Posting Number A2026037P Specialty and subspecialty, if any, sought Interior Design / Interior Architecture / Adaptive Reuse Courses to be covered HPCP 340: Special Topics in Historic Preservation Position Description The Historic Preservation & Community Planning Program in the Department of Art and Architectural History at the College of Charleston invites applications for an Adjunct Instructor to teach HPCP 340: Special Topics in Historic Preservation during Spring 2027. The course will be an Interior Design Studio. HPCP 340: Special Topics, Interior Design Studio is a three-credit undergraduate studio course focused on the principles and practice of interior design within historic and existing buildings. The course will meet in person once each week for one three-hour studio session on the College of Charleston campus. The course introduces students to space planning, circulation, human dimensions, accessibility, scale and proportion, color, lighting, materials and finishes, furnishings, visual communication, and the relationship between contemporary interior interventions and existing architectural character. Particular emphasis is placed on adaptive reuse and the sensitive treatment of historic interiors for contemporary use . The instructor will guide students through field investigation, precedent study, measured documentation, programming, schematic design, materials and lighting development, drawing and digital representation, model making, individual desk critiques, group pin-ups, and formal design reviews. The semester will culminate in a comprehensive adaptive-reuse interior design project. We seek an instructor who can bring professional design practice into the studio and mentor undergraduate students through an iterative design process. The successful candidate should demonstrate expertise in interior design, interior architecture, architecture, historic preservation, adaptive reuse, or a closely related field, as well as strong visual communication and design-development skills. Experience with historic buildings, rehabilitation, adaptive reuse, accessibility, lighting, materials, furniture and finishes, or interior detailing is particularly desirable. Familiarity with contemporary design and visualization tools such as AutoCAD, SketchUp, Adobe Creative Suite, or comparable software is preferred. Previous college-level teaching experience is desirable but not required. Professional practice, mentoring, studio critique, internship supervision, or comparable experience working with emerging designers will be considered valuable. A master’s degree in Interior Design, Interior Architecture, Architecture, Historic Preservation, or a closely related field is preferred. Candidates with other graduate preparation and substantial professional expertise directly relevant to the course may be considered subject to College of Charleston faculty credentialing requirements. Applicants must demonstrate professional or academic expertise relevant to interior design and the ability to communicate design concepts effectively to undergraduate students. This is a part-time, non-tenure-track adjunct appointment for Spring 2027. Appointment is subject to College of Charleston faculty credentialing requirements, sufficient enrollment, available funding, and successful completion of required employment procedures. Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants should submit the following through the College of Charleston PeopleAdmin system: A letter of application describing interest in teaching HPCP 340: Special Topics, Interior Design Studio and relevant professional and/or teaching experience; A current CV or résumé; Unofficial graduate transcript(s); A professional portfolio or portfolio website demonstrating relevant design work; and Names and contact information for three professional references. Teaching evaluations or examples of prior teaching may be included if available but are not required. Review of applications will begin 16 November and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to: Dr. R. Grant Gilmore III Director, Historic Preservation & Community Planning College of Charleston gilmorerg@cofc.edu Is this position Grant funded? Yes EEO Statement The College of Charleston is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against any individual or group on the basis of sex, gender (including gender identity and/or expression), pregnancy, race, religion, color, national origin, age, disability, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, genetic information, and other classifications protected by applicable federal, state, and local laws. For more information, please visit eop.cofc.edu . Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.cofc.edu/postings/18726 Reference Letters Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Chronicle of Higher Education CofC - Alumni Career Services College of Charleston Website Diverse: Issues in Higher Education Glassdoor Handshake HigherEdJobs Indeed.com Internal Job Posting jbcjobs (Joint Base Charleston Military & Family Readiness) Job Fair LinkedIn Monster.com Personal Referral Post and Courier Public Job Posting SC Works (SC Department of Employment and Workforce) Twitter Word of mouth Other National Labor Exchange CareerBuilder SpouseWorks Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter / Letter of Application Optional Documents Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Research and Professional Development Statement Unofficial Undergraduate Transcripts Unofficial Graduate Transcripts Sample Publication 1 Sample Publication 2 Sample Publication 3 Student Course-Instructor Reviews Slideshow of Personal Work (limited to 20 images) Slideshow of Student Work (limited to 20 images) Teaching Portfolio Reference List Email: jobs@cofc.edu General Information: 843.953.5512 Mailing Address: 66 George St. Charleston, SC 29424 HR Office 160 Calhoun St. Lightsey Building B36 Charleston, SC 29424 Campus Maps ©2020 All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.cofc.edu/postings/18797": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor and Academic Program Director of Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "College of Charleston Employment Site | Associate/Full Professor and Academic Program Director of Mechanical Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Applicant Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Academic Jobs Student Jobs Contact Us Associate/Full Professor and Academic Program Director of Mechanical Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin on 12/1/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Posting Details POSTING INFORMATION Internal Title Associate/Full Professor and Academic Program Director of Mechanical Engineering Position Type Tenure-track School/Area School of Engineering, Computer Science, and Mathematics Department Engineering Position Description The School of Engineering, Computing, and Mathematics ( SECM ) at the College of Charleston invites applications for a tenure-track or tenured faculty position to serve as the founding Academic Program Director of Mechanical Engineering at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor, with an anticipated start date of August 2027. Appointment rank, tenure status, and salary will be commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications and experience. Teaching and research are mutually supportive activities at the College of Charleston. The successful applicant will have a plan for bringing a research program to the College. Relevant areas include heat transfer, thermal fluids, fluid mechanics, machine design, mechanics of materials, materials science, robotics, mechatronics, instrumentation and controls, combustion, or other relevant mechanical engineering topics. The successful candidate will provide academic leadership in the development, implementation, and continuous improvement of a new Bachelor of Science (B.S.) program in Mechanical Engineering. This position presents a unique opportunity for an entrepreneurial educator and scholar to lead the creation of a distinctive mechanical engineering program at a rapidly growing public university located in one of the nation’s most vibrant manufacturing and technology corridors. This is an exciting time to join the University as it continues strong enrollment growth and is strategically investing to increase national prominence. The Program Director will work collaboratively with engineering faculty, faculties across campus, administrators across the College, and external stakeholders to develop and launch the Mechanical Engineering program. The successful candidate will lead efforts to establish program educational objectives, student outcomes, curriculum, laboratories, assessment processes, industry partnerships, and an advisory board necessary to support future accreditation by the Engineering Accreditation Commission ( EAC ) of ABET . Established in 2023 as part of this new School, the Department of Engineering currently offers Bachelor of Science degrees in Electrical Engineering and Systems Engineering, Integrated Engineering and Business, and a Bachelor of Arts in General Engineering. The department is continuing its strategic expansion by the addition of both Biomedical Engineering and Mechanical Engineering programs. The addition of Mechanical Engineering represents a major strategic initiative for the College and aligns with opportunities for expanding collaboration with the substantial manufacturing base in Charleston, regional engineering activities, and South Carolina’s growing automotive, aerospace, energy, defense, and advanced manufacturing industries. SECM offers a friendly, dynamic, and collaborative environment, with ample opportunities for interdisciplinary partnerships across the university’s ten schools. The university is located a short drive from Boeing, Volvo, Mercedes-Benz Vans facilities, Joint Base Charleston, Naval Information Warfare Center ( NWIC ) Atlantic, and the defense ecosystem surrounding, and more, providing exceptional opportunities for collaboration in mechanical engineering research, innovation, and student experiential learning. Appointment as Academic Program Director is expected upon hire and is accompanied by teaching load reassignment to support program leadership and administrative responsibilities. The Program Director appointment is a renewable administrative assignment reporting to the Department Chair of Engineering and is subject to College policies and procedures. Major Responsibilities · Provide leadership in the establishment, growth, and continuous improvement of the Mechanical Engineering B.S. program. · Lead curriculum development and implementation consistent with institutional and ABET accreditation requirements. · Mentor and provide professional development for junior faculty in Mechanical Engineering. · Develop and maintain program educational objectives, student outcomes, assessment processes, and continuous improvement plans. · Lead and coordinate preparation for initial and continuing ABET accreditation. · Coordinate course scheduling, enrollment planning, and instructional staffing for the Mechanical Engineering program. · Recruit, advise, and mentor Mechanical Engineering students. · Collaborate with Engineering Department leadership regarding faculty hiring needs, resource planning, and laboratory development. · Develop partnerships with aerospace, automotive, and advanced manufacturing industries, and other external stakeholders. · Establish and maintain an effective Industrial Advisory Board. · Prepare annual reports, strategic plans, and budget requests for the program. · Promote student success through experiential learning, undergraduate research, internships, and industry engagement. · Maintain an active program of scholarship and participate in service activities consistent with faculty rank and expectations. · Teach undergraduate engineering courses. Required Qualifications · Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering discipline. · Proven track record of teaching, advising, and mentoring students. · Evidence of excellence in academic research and scholarly achievement appropriate for appointment at the rank sought. · Academic qualifications sufficient to teach core mechanical engineering coursework, including thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, and heat transfer. · Experience with engineering curriculum development. · Knowledge of ABET accreditation processes and requirements. · Strong written, organizational, and interpersonal communication skills. · Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across disciplines and academic units. Additional Qualifications by Rank · Associate Professor: Record of teaching effectiveness, scholarly achievement, and service consistent with appointment at the associate professor level. The ability to be granted tenure at time of hire preferred. · Professor: Distinguished record of teaching, scholarship, and leadership sufficient for appointment with tenure at the professor rank. Preferred Qualifications · Experience developing, coordinating, or directing an academic program. · Experience with ABET self-study preparation, accreditation reviews, or continuous improvement processes. · Experience in heat transfer, thermal fluids, fluid mechanics, machine design, mechanics of materials, materials science, robotics, mechatronics, instrumentation and controls, combustion, or related areas. · Experience developing mechanical engineering teaching and research laboratories. · Experience collaborating with government or industry partners. · Experience securing externally funded research, educational, or workforce development programs. · Interdisciplinary experience spanning engineering and mechanical engineering fields. · Possessing an ABET accredited B.S. Degree in Mechanical Engineering · Post-doctoral and/or industry experience · Experience with relevant regulatory processes and standards, as appropriate (e.g. OSHA , ASTM , SAE , etc.) · Professional Engineer (PE) licensure Application Instructions Qualified candidates should submit: · Letter of application addressing qualifications for the position and interest in developing a new Mechanical Engineering program. · Curriculum vitae. · Statement of teaching philosophy. · Statement of research and scholarly interests. · Statement describing experience with program development, accreditation, academic leadership, or related activities. · Names and contact information for three references. For additional information, contact: Dr. G. Wes Hitt Chair, Mechanical Engineering Program Director Search Committee Department of Engineering School of Engineering, Computing, and Mathematics College of Charleston 66 George Street Charleston, SC 29424 Email: hittw@cofc.edu About the College of Charleston and the School of Engineering, Computing, and Mathematics: Founded in 1770, the College of Charleston is a comprehensive public university and committed to developing intellectually versatile and globally fluent citizens who create innovative solutions to social, economic, and technological challenges. A primarily undergraduate institution with strong graduate degree programs, the university prides itself on excellence in education. The university enrolls over 10,000 undergraduate and 1000 graduate students. In its 2025 edition, U.S. News & World Report ranks the College as the No. 4 top public university in the south, the No. 5 ranked college for veterans among southern institutions, the No. 9 most innovative school in the region, and the No. 3 best college for undergraduate teaching in the south and a best value regional college. The College is in the heart of beautiful Charleston. Visit the College’s website at https://www.cofc.edu . Visit the School’s website at https://charleston.edu/school-engineering-computing-mathematics/ . Special Instructions to Applicants Review of applications will begin on 12/1/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Posting Date 10/30/2026 Closing Date Open Until Filled? Yes Posting Number F2026026P EEO Statement The College of Charleston is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against any individual or group on the basis of sex, gender (including gender identity and/or expression), pregnancy, race, religion, color, national origin, age, disability, military or veteran status, sexual orientation, genetic information, and other classifications protected by applicable federal, state, and local laws. For more information, please visit eop.cofc.edu . Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.cofc.edu/postings/18766 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Chronicle of Higher Education CofC - Alumni Career Services College of Charleston Website Diverse: Issues in Higher Education Glassdoor Handshake HigherEdJobs Indeed.com Internal Job Posting jbcjobs (Joint Base Charleston Military & Family Readiness) Job Fair LinkedIn Monster.com Personal Referral Post and Courier Public Job Posting SC Works (SC Department of Employment and Workforce) Twitter Word of mouth Other National Labor Exchange CareerBuilder SpouseWorks Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Research and Professional Development Statement Reference List Statement of Leadership Philosophy Optional Documents Form DD 214 Email: jobs@cofc.edu General Information: 843.953.5512 Mailing Address: 66 George St. Charleston, SC 29424 HR Office 160 Calhoun St. Lightsey Building B36 Charleston, SC 29424 Campus Maps ©2020 All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
